% 38d-emit-x86.weft — The second target: an x86-64 / Mach-O back end that
% emits textual assembly and delegates encoding to the system assembler.

\chapter{A Second Target: x86-64 by Assembly Emission}
\label{ch:emit-x86}

The flagship ARM64 back end (Chapters~\ref{ch:emit-arm64}--\ref{ch:emit-macho})
proves \kcc{} can go all the way to the metal: it encodes every instruction
into its own bytes and lays out the Mach-O container by hand.  The second
target takes the other road the porting playbook
(Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting}) sanctions---\emph{assembly emission}.  Instead
of encoding instructions and writing an object file, this back end walks the
same target-neutral IR the ARM64 path consumes and \emph{prints x86-64
assembly text}, then hands that text to the platform assembler
(\texttt{as -arch x86\_64}).  The assembler does the encoding and produces the
Mach-O object; the linker (\texttt{ld -arch x86\_64}) produces an executable
that runs on Apple silicon under the Rosetta translator.  Nothing above the
emission step---the front end, the IR, the optimizer---knows or cares that a
different machine is now the destination; that is the whole point of the seam.

This chapter is built one rung of the playbook ladder at a time.  Rung~one
(\S\ref{sec:x86-rung1-history}) was the smallest program that exercises the
entire pipeline end to end, \verb|int main(void) { return N; }|, whose
optimized IR is a single \texttt{IR\_CONST} feeding an \texttt{IR\_RET}.  With
the scaffolding proven live, this chapter is \emph{rung~two}: the integer
arithmetic--logic unit plus the memory operations that give locals an address.
That is exactly the set of opcodes a function like

\begin{verbatim}
  int f(void) {
      volatile int x = 40;
      volatile int y = 2;
      return x + y;
  }
\end{verbatim}

\noindent lowers to---\texttt{IR\_ALLOCA} for each local, \texttt{IR\_STORE}
to initialise it, \texttt{IR\_LOAD} to read it back (\texttt{volatile} stops
\texttt{mem2reg} from promoting the locals into registers, so the memory
traffic survives to the back end), \texttt{IR\_ADD} to combine them, and an
\texttt{IR\_SEXT} feeding the return.  Rung~two selects for the whole integer
ALU (add, subtract, multiply, divide, remainder, the bitwise and shift
operators, negate and complement), the width-changing conversions
(\texttt{sext}/\texttt{zext}/\texttt{trunc}/\texttt{bitcast}), and the memory
opcodes (\texttt{alloca}/\texttt{load}/\texttt{store}/\texttt{gep}).

\emph{Rung~three} completes the scalar language: control flow and function
calls.  It adds the comparisons (\texttt{cmp\_eq} through \texttt{cmp\_ule}),
the branches (\texttt{br}/\texttt{br\_cond}), and the call machinery
(\texttt{call}/\texttt{call\_ind}/\texttt{global\_ref}) marshalled to the
System~V AMD64 ABI, along with the callee-side spill of incoming parameters.
With this rung a whole program of mutually recursive integer functions,
conditionals, and loops compiles and runs exit-identically to clang, provided
its data lives in \emph{functions}: parameters, locals, and calls between
defined functions all work, and an \texttt{IR\_GLOBAL\_REF} to a defined
\emph{function} resolves (that is how an indirect call names its target).
What this rung deliberately leaves out is \emph{data emission}: it emits no
storage for file-scope variables, so a reference to a global object is an
unresolved symbol at link time rather than a miscompile.  That, together with
string-literal data, floating point, and by-value aggregates, is the work of a
later rung; an unrecognised opcode is still silently skipped.

\section{Strategy: an All-Spill Frame}
\label{sec:x86-strategy}

The x86-64 back end borrows the ARM64 code generator's ``all-spill'' strategy
(\S\ref{sec:codegen-strategy}) wholesale, because it is the simplest thing that
is correct and it sidesteps register allocation entirely.  \emph{Every virtual
register owns one eight-byte slot in the stack frame.}  An instruction loads
its operands from their slots into scratch registers, computes, and stores the
result back to the destination's slot.  No value is assumed to survive in a
register from one IR instruction to the next, so there is no allocation, no
liveness, and no spilling logic---just a fixed, mechanical lowering.

The frame is the conventional \texttt{\%rbp}-anchored one and has two regions
below the saved base pointer:

\begin{verbatim}
  0(%rbp)                 saved %rbp (pushq'd on entry)
  -8(%rbp) .. -vc*8(%rbp)  vreg value slots   (vreg_count * 8)
  below that              alloca storage      (alloca_slots * 8)
\end{verbatim}

Virtual register~$V$ lives at \verb|-(V+1)*8(%rbp)|.  Alloca storage begins
past the value slots so an \texttt{alloca}'s object can never overlap a vreg
slot (the same anti-aliasing discipline the ARM64 frame uses).  A pre-scan
assigns each \texttt{IR\_ALLOCA} a running slot offset and sums the total; the
frame is \verb|align16((vreg_count + alloca_slots) * 8)| bytes, carved out with
one \texttt{subq} in the prologue and released by \texttt{leave} at each return.

Arithmetic runs at full 64-bit width, exactly as the ARM64 path does: operands
arrive in their slots already sign- or zero-extended (the loads and the
explicit conversion opcodes see to that), so a 64-bit \texttt{addq} computes
the correct low bits and the IR's own \texttt{sext}/\texttt{trunc} nodes
re-normalise a narrower result where the language requires it.  The scratch
registers are \verb|%r10| (first source and result), \verb|%r11| (second
source), and---only for the shift and divide instructions, which pin specific
registers---\verb|%rcx|, \verb|%rax|, and \verb|%rdx|.

\section{Target State and Assembly Vocabulary}
\label{sec:x86-data}

A single global, \texttt{\_kcc\_target}, records which back end the driver
selected: \texttt{0} (the default) is ARM64/Mach-O and leaves the flagship
path completely untouched; \texttt{1} is x86-64.  A second file-local global,
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_vreg\_n}, holds the current function's virtual-register
count so instruction selection can size the frame and compute alloca
addresses.  The remaining data are the fixed fragments of AT\&T assembly this
back end pours into the text buffer---section and symbol directives, the frame
prologue/epilogue, every instruction mnemonic, and the register operands.
AT\&T syntax names registers with a \texttt{\%} sigil, immediates with
\texttt{\$}, orders operands source-then-destination, and writes a memory
operand as \verb|disp(base)|.

@d X86: data @{
.data
.globl _kcc_target
.globl _kcc_x86_tu
_kcc_target:     .quad 0        // 0 = arm64/Mach-O (default), 1 = x86-64
_kcc_x86_vreg_n: .quad 0        // current function's vreg count (frame sizing)
_kcc_x86_tu:     .quad 0        // the AST translation unit (for data emission)
_kcc_x86_strs:   .quad 0        // vec of {data_ptr, len} string-literal entries

.section __TEXT,__cstring,cstring_literals
// Directives and symbol scaffolding.
_kcc_x86_s_text:     .asciz ".text\n"
_kcc_x86_s_globl:    .asciz ".globl _"
_kcc_x86_s_us:       .asciz "_"
_kcc_x86_s_colon_nl: .asciz ":\n"
_kcc_x86_s_minus:    .asciz "-"
_kcc_x86_s_rbp:      .asciz "(%rbp)"
_kcc_x86_s_comma:    .asciz ", "

// Frame prologue/epilogue.
_kcc_x86_s_push_rbp: .asciz "\tpushq\t%rbp\n"
_kcc_x86_s_mov_sp:   .asciz "\tmovq\t%rsp, %rbp\n"
_kcc_x86_s_sub_sp:   .asciz "\tsubq\t$"
_kcc_x86_s_sp_nl:    .asciz ", %rsp\n"
_kcc_x86_s_leave:    .asciz "\tleave\n"
_kcc_x86_s_retq:     .asciz "\tretq\n"

// Move family (with a trailing tab where an operand follows).
_kcc_x86_s_movq:     .asciz "\tmovq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movabsq:  .asciz "\tmovabsq\t$"
_kcc_x86_s_movl:     .asciz "\tmovl\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movb:     .asciz "\tmovb\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movw:     .asciz "\tmovw\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movslq:   .asciz "\tmovslq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movsbq:   .asciz "\tmovsbq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movswq:   .asciz "\tmovswq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movzbq:   .asciz "\tmovzbq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movzwq:   .asciz "\tmovzwq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_leaq:     .asciz "\tleaq\t"

// Integer ALU mnemonics.
_kcc_x86_s_addq:     .asciz "\taddq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_subq:     .asciz "\tsubq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_imulq:    .asciz "\timulq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_andq:     .asciz "\tandq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_orq:      .asciz "\torq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_xorq:     .asciz "\txorq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_negq:     .asciz "\tnegq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_notq:     .asciz "\tnotq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_shlq:     .asciz "\tshlq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_sarq:     .asciz "\tsarq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_shrq:     .asciz "\tshrq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_cqto:     .asciz "\tcqto\n"
_kcc_x86_s_xor_rdx:  .asciz "\txorq\t%rdx, %rdx\n"
_kcc_x86_s_idiv_rcx: .asciz "\tidivq\t%rcx\n"
_kcc_x86_s_div_rcx:  .asciz "\tdivq\t%rcx\n"

// Register operands and common instruction tails.
_kcc_x86_s_r10:      .asciz "%r10"
_kcc_x86_s_r11:      .asciz "%r11"
_kcc_x86_s_rax:      .asciz "%rax"
_kcc_x86_s_rcx:      .asciz "%rcx"
_kcc_x86_s_rdx:      .asciz "%rdx"
_kcc_x86_s_r11_r10:  .asciz "%r11, %r10\n"   // binary op tail: dst = dst OP r11
_kcc_x86_s_r10_nl:   .asciz "%r10\n"          // unary op tail
_kcc_x86_s_cl_r10:   .asciz "%cl, %r10\n"     // shift-by-%cl tail
_kcc_x86_s_c_r10:    .asciz ", %r10\n"        // ", %r10\n"
_kcc_x86_s_c_r10d:   .asciz ", %r10d\n"       // ", %r10d\n"
// Memory operand tails for load (source (%r11)) and store (dest (%r11)).
_kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10: .asciz "(%r11), %r10\n"
_kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10d:.asciz "(%r11), %r10d\n"
_kcc_x86_s_r10_pr11: .asciz "%r10, (%r11)\n"
_kcc_x86_s_r10b_pr11:.asciz "%r10b, (%r11)\n"
_kcc_x86_s_r10w_pr11:.asciz "%r10w, (%r11)\n"
_kcc_x86_s_r10d_pr11:.asciz "%r10d, (%r11)\n"
@}

\subsection{Vocabulary for Control Flow and Calls (Rung 3)}
\label{sec:x86-data-rung3}

Rung~three adds the comparison, branch, and call vocabulary.  Comparisons emit
a \texttt{cmp} at the operands' common width followed by a \texttt{set}\emph{cc}
into \verb|%al| and a \texttt{movzbl} that widens the boolean into
\verb|%r10|.  Branches are the great simplification of assembly emission: a
basic block becomes a named label \verb|.Lkccbb|$N$ (block ids are globally
unique), so an \texttt{IR\_BR} is a \texttt{jmp} to that label and the
assembler resolves forward and backward references itself---no patch vector.
Calls follow the System~V AMD64 ABI: the integer argument registers, in order,
are \verb|%rdi|, \verb|%rsi|, \verb|%rdx|, \verb|%rcx|, \verb|%r8|, \verb|%r9|
(collected in \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_argregs} for index-based lookup), the return
value comes back in \verb|%rax|, and a direct call names the callee with the
Mach-O leading underscore.

@d X86: data @{
// Comparison: cmp at width, set<cc> into %al, widen with movzbl.
_kcc_x86_s_cmpl:     .asciz "\tcmpl\t%r11d, %r10d\n"
_kcc_x86_s_cmpq:     .asciz "\tcmpq\t%r11, %r10\n"
_kcc_x86_s_movzbl_al:.asciz "\tmovzbl\t%al, %r10d\n"
_kcc_x86_s_sete:     .asciz "\tsete\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setne:    .asciz "\tsetne\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setl:     .asciz "\tsetl\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setle:    .asciz "\tsetle\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setg:     .asciz "\tsetg\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setge:    .asciz "\tsetge\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setb:     .asciz "\tsetb\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setbe:    .asciz "\tsetbe\t%al\n"

// Branches: named basic-block labels resolved by the assembler.
_kcc_x86_s_bb:       .asciz ".Lkccbb"        // label stem; a decimal id follows
_kcc_x86_s_label_nl: .asciz ":\n"
_kcc_x86_s_jmp:      .asciz "\tjmp\t"
_kcc_x86_s_jne:      .asciz "\tjne\t"
_kcc_x86_s_test_r10: .asciz "\ttestq\t%r10, %r10\n"

// Calls: System V AMD64 argument registers, call forms, and stack adjust.
_kcc_x86_s_rdi:      .asciz "%rdi"
_kcc_x86_s_rsi:      .asciz "%rsi"
_kcc_x86_s_r8:       .asciz "%r8"
_kcc_x86_s_r9:       .asciz "%r9"
_kcc_x86_s_call:     .asciz "\tcall\t"
_kcc_x86_s_call_r11: .asciz "\tcall\t*%r11\n"
_kcc_x86_s_zero_al:  .asciz "\tmovb\t$0, %al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_sub_rsp:  .asciz "\tsubq\t$"
_kcc_x86_s_add_rsp:  .asciz "\taddq\t$"
_kcc_x86_s_rsp:      .asciz ", %rsp\n"
_kcc_x86_s_paren_rsp:.asciz "(%rsp)"
_kcc_x86_s_rip_r10:  .asciz "(%rip), %r10\n"
_kcc_x86_s_got_r10:  .asciz "@@GOTPCREL(%rip), %r10\n"

// Data emission: zeroed common storage for a file-scope global.
_kcc_x86_s_zerofill: .asciz "\t.zerofill __DATA,__common,_"
_kcc_x86_s_zf_a3:    .asciz ",3\n"      // ",<size>,3\n" alignment tail (8-byte)
_kcc_x86_s_comma1:   .asciz ","

// Refusal diagnostic for an opcode the back end does not yet implement.
_kcc_x86_s_unsup:    .asciz "kcc: --target=x86_64: unsupported operation (floating point, inline asm, or by-value aggregate not yet implemented for the x86-64 back end)\n"

// String literals: a private label per literal, its address via leaq, and the
// cstring section that holds the bytes.
_kcc_x86_s_strlbl:   .asciz ".Lx86str"      // label stem; a decimal id follows
_kcc_x86_s_cstrsect: .asciz ".section __TEXT,__cstring,cstring_literals\n"
_kcc_x86_s_byte:     .asciz "\t.byte "
_kcc_x86_s_zero_nl:  .asciz "0\n"

// The argument-register lookup table holds pointers, so it lives in a
// relocatable data section, not the cstring pool (which admits no relocations).
.data
.p2align 3
_kcc_x86_argregs:
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_rdi
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_rsi
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_rdx
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_rcx
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_r8
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_r9
@}

\section{Text-Buffer Emission Helpers}
\label{sec:x86-helpers}

Selection is written in terms of a handful of primitives over the growable
text buffer (\S\ref{sec:emit-asm-buffer}): \texttt{\_kcc\_as\_str} appends a
NUL-terminated string, \texttt{\_kcc\_as\_ch} one byte, and
\texttt{\_kcc\_as\_dec} an unsigned decimal.  On top of those,
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_disp} prints a frame displacement \verb|-D(%rbp)|, and
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_slot} prints the operand for virtual register~$V$, whose
displacement is $(V+1)\times 8$.  \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_load} emits
\verb|movq slot(V), reg| (a slot always holds a canonical 64-bit value, so a
plain \texttt{movq} reads any vreg), and \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_store} the inverse
\verb|movq reg, slot(V)|.  A register operand is passed by pointer to its name
string.

@d X86: render @{
.text
// -- kcc_x86_disp(uint64_t d) — append "-d(%rbp)" (a frame displacement).
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_disp:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    str x19, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // d
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_minus@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_minus@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rbp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rbp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x19, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_slot(uint64_t v) — append the operand for vreg v: "-(v+1)*8(%rbp)".
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_slot:
    add x0, x0, #1
    lsl x0, x0, #3
    b _kcc_x86_disp

// -- kcc_x86_load(uint64_t v, const char *reg) — emit "movq slot(v), reg".
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_load:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // v
    mov x20, x1                     // reg name
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_x86_slot
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, #10                     // '\n'
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_store(uint64_t v, const char *reg) — emit "movq reg, slot(v)".
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_store:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // v
    mov x20, x1                     // reg name
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_x86_slot
    mov x0, #10                     // '\n'
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\section{Instruction Selection}
\label{sec:x86-sel}

Selection is a per-instruction dispatch: given one IR instruction, emit the
equivalent x86-64 and leave the result in the destination's slot.  The
opcode routes to a handler; anything rung~two does not yet implement (control
flow, floating point) falls through and emits nothing.  We build the handlers
bottom-up as a set of small helpers---one per instruction \emph{shape}---then
the dispatch that calls them.

\subsection{Binary, Unary, and Shift Shapes}
\label{sec:x86-alu}

The commutative and two-operand ALU instructions all share one shape: load
\texttt{SRC1} into \verb|%r10|, load \texttt{SRC2} into \verb|%r11|, emit the
mnemonic followed by \verb|%r11, %r10| (so \verb|%r10| becomes
\verb|%r10 OP %r11|), and store \verb|%r10| to \texttt{DEST}.  Subtraction is
non-commutative but the operand order is already correct
(\verb|subq %r11, %r10| is $\mathtt{src1}-\mathtt{src2}$).  The unary shape
(negate, complement) omits the second load.  The shift shape loads the count
into \verb|%rcx| because x86 variable shifts take their amount only in
\verb|%cl|.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_binop(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem) — dst = src1 OP src2.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_binop:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // mnemonic
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_r11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_r11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_unop(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem) — dst = OP src1.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_unop:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0
    mov x20, x1
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_shift(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem) — dst = src1 SHIFT src2 (%cl).
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_shift:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0
    mov x20, x1
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rcx@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rcx@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cl_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cl_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\subsection{Division and Remainder}
\label{sec:x86-div}

x86 division is the one integer operation with fixed register operands.  The
dividend goes in \verb|%rdx:%rax|; \texttt{idivq}/\texttt{divq} leaves the
quotient in \verb|%rax| and the remainder in \verb|%rdx|.  The helper loads
\texttt{SRC1} into \verb|%rax|, widens it into \verb|%rdx| (\texttt{cqto}
sign-extends for the signed forms, \verb|xorq %rdx, %rdx| zeroes it for the
unsigned ones), loads the divisor into \verb|%rcx|, divides, and stores either
\verb|%rax| (quotient, for \texttt{sdiv}/\texttt{udiv}) or \verb|%rdx|
(remainder, for \texttt{smod}/\texttt{umod}) to \texttt{DEST}.  Because the
operands were sign- or zero-extended when they were loaded from memory, the
full-width division agrees with C's trunc-toward-zero semantics on the narrow
type.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_divop(ir_instr *i, uint64_t is_signed, uint64_t want_rem)
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_divop:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // is_signed
    mov x21, x2                     // want_rem
    // %rax = src1
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    // Widen the dividend into %rdx.
    cbz x20, .Lx86div_unsigned
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cqto@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cqto@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    b .Lx86div_divisor
.Lx86div_unsigned:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_xor_rdx@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_xor_rdx@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86div_divisor:
    // %rcx = src2
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rcx@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rcx@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    // idivq / divq %rcx
    cbz x20, .Lx86div_unsigned_op
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_idiv_rcx@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_idiv_rcx@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    b .Lx86div_result
.Lx86div_unsigned_op:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_div_rcx@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_div_rcx@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86div_result:
    // Store %rax (quotient) or %rdx (remainder) to DEST.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    cbnz x21, .Lx86div_rem
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86div_store
.Lx86div_rem:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rdx@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rdx@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86div_store:
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret
@}

\subsection{Width-Changing Conversions}
\label{sec:x86-conv}

The four conversions all read \texttt{SRC1} straight from its slot with a
single extending move---the low bytes of the canonical slot value are exactly
the narrow source---and store the widened result.
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_extend} takes the mnemonic and the destination-register
tail (\verb|, %r10| for the 64-bit forms, \verb|, %r10d| for \texttt{movl},
which zero-extends into \verb|%r10|) and emits \verb|mnem slot(SRC1), reg|.
\texttt{IR\_SEXT} and \texttt{IR\_ZEXT} choose the mnemonic from their source
width in \texttt{SRC2} (\texttt{movsbq}/\texttt{movswq}/\texttt{movslq} signed,
\texttt{movzbq}/\texttt{movzwq}/\texttt{movl} unsigned); \texttt{IR\_TRUNC}
chooses from its \emph{target} width in \texttt{IR\_TYPE} and zero-extends the
surviving low bytes, matching the ARM64 mask-and-copy; \texttt{IR\_BITCAST} is
a plain \texttt{movq} copy.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_extend(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem, const char *tail)
//    Emit "mnem slot(SRC1)tail" then store %r10 to DEST.  `tail` supplies the
//    ", %r10\n" (or ", %r10d\n") destination-register text.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_extend:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    str x21, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // mnemonic
    mov x21, x2                     // tail
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    bl _kcc_x86_slot
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldr x21, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_conv(ir_instr *i, uint64_t width, uint64_t is_signed)
//    Pick the extending mnemonic/tail for a `width`-byte source and emit it.
//    is_signed selects movs* vs movz*/movl; width 8 or 0 is a plain movq copy.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_conv:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x1                     // width
    mov x20, x2                     // is_signed
    // x0 already holds instr; stash it so the dispatch below can reload.
    str x0, [sp, #-16]!            // save instr (16-byte aligned push)
    cmp x19, #1
    b.eq .Lx86conv_b
    cmp x19, #2
    b.eq .Lx86conv_w
    cmp x19, #4
    b.eq .Lx86conv_l
    // Default (8 or unknown): movq copy, no extension.
    ldr x0, [sp], #16
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_extend
    b .Lx86conv_done
.Lx86conv_b:
    ldr x0, [sp], #16
    cbz x20, .Lx86conv_bz
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movsbq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movsbq@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86conv_b_go
.Lx86conv_bz:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzbq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzbq@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86conv_b_go:
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_extend
    b .Lx86conv_done
.Lx86conv_w:
    ldr x0, [sp], #16
    cbz x20, .Lx86conv_wz
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movswq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movswq@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86conv_w_go
.Lx86conv_wz:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzwq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzwq@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86conv_w_go:
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_extend
    b .Lx86conv_done
.Lx86conv_l:
    ldr x0, [sp], #16
    cbz x20, .Lx86conv_lz
    // Signed 32→64: movslq slot, %r10.
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movslq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movslq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_extend
    b .Lx86conv_done
.Lx86conv_lz:
    // Unsigned 32→64: movl slot, %r10d (implicitly zero-extends into %r10).
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10d@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10d@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_extend
.Lx86conv_done:
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\subsection{Memory Operations}
\label{sec:x86-mem}

\texttt{IR\_LOAD} dereferences a pointer with an access-size override in
\texttt{SRC2} (the code $1/2/4$ unsigned, $5/6/7$ signed, $0$ eight-byte;
$8$ is a single-precision float, deferred to a later rung).  The helper loads
the pointer into \verb|%r11|, emits the width-appropriate move from
\verb|(%r11)| into \verb|%r10| (or \verb|%r10d| for the zero-extending
32-bit \texttt{movl}), and stores \verb|%r10| to \texttt{DEST}.
\texttt{IR\_STORE} is the inverse with its size override in \texttt{IR\_DEST}
($0/1/2/4$): load the value into \verb|%r10| and the pointer into \verb|%r11|,
then \verb|mov %r10x, (%r11)| at the right width.  \texttt{IR\_GEP} (get
element pointer) is the address arithmetic that array subscripts and struct
member accesses lower to: its second operand is already a \emph{byte} offset
(the IR builder pre-scales a subscript by the element size), so the address is
just \texttt{SRC1}${}+{}$\texttt{SRC2}---the very shape of a two-operand ALU
add, and so it reuses \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_binop} with \texttt{addq}.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_memload(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem, const char *tail)
//    Emit "mov* (%r11), reg" for a pointer already implied by SRC1, storing to
//    DEST.  Loads the pointer into %r11 first.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_memload:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    str x21, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // mnemonic
    mov x21, x2                     // tail  "(%r11), %r10\n" etc.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldr x21, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_memstore(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem, const char *valtail)
//    Emit "mov* reg, (%r11)".  Loads value into %r10, pointer into %r11.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_memstore:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    str x21, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0
    mov x20, x1                     // mnemonic
    mov x21, x2                     // valtail  "%r10, (%r11)\n" etc.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // value
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // pointer
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x21, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret
@}

\subsection{Comparison, Label, and Call Helpers (Rung 3)}
\label{sec:x86-rung3-helpers}

Three more shape helpers cover control flow.  \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_cmp} emits a
comparison: it loads both operands, compares them at the width recorded in the
instruction's type (32-bit \texttt{cmpl} for an \texttt{int}-or-narrower common
type, else 64-bit \texttt{cmpq}), materialises the boolean with the caller's
chosen \texttt{set}\emph{cc} into \verb|%al|, widens it into \verb|%r10| with
\texttt{movzbl}, and stores it.  \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_bblabel} appends a
basic-block label reference \verb|.Lkccbb|$N$ (used both to define a block's
label and to name a branch target).  \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_marshal} realises the
System~V argument-passing rules for the integer class: it reads the argument
array (\texttt{[count\,|\,fsi{<}{<}32, vreg0, class0, \ldots]}), places
arguments seven and beyond into a freshly subtracted stack region at
\verb|(k-6)*8(%rsp)|, loads the first six into \verb|%rdi|--\verb|%r9|, and
returns the \texttt{stack\_adj} the caller adds back after the call.
Floating-point and by-value aggregate arguments are a later rung; rung~three
treats every argument as integer/pointer.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_cmp(ir_instr *i, const char *setcc) — dst = (src1 CMP src2) ? 1:0.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_cmp:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // set<cc> string
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    // Compare width: <=4-byte common type → 32-bit cmpl, else 64-bit cmpq.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]
    cbz x0, .Lx86cmp_q
    ldr x0, [x0, #TY_SIZE]
    cmp x0, #8
    b.eq .Lx86cmp_q
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cmpl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cmpl@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86cmp_emit
.Lx86cmp_q:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cmpq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cmpq@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86cmp_emit:
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20                     // set<cc> %al
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movzbl_al@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movzbl_al@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_bblabel(uint64_t bb_id) — append ".Lkccbb<id>".
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_bblabel:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    str x19, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_bb@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_bb@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    ldr x19, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_marshal(uint64_t *args, uint64_t count) → stack_adj.
//    Loads integer/pointer args into %rdi..%r9 and args 7+ onto the stack.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_marshal:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // args array
    mov x20, x1                     // count
    mov x22, #0                     // stack_adj (return value)
    cbz x20, .Lx86mar_done

    // Stack phase: if count > 6, reserve align16((count-6)*8) and store the
    // overflow args (index 6..count-1) at (k-6)*8(%rsp).
    cmp x20, #6
    b.le .Lx86mar_regs
    sub x0, x20, #6
    lsl x0, x0, #3
    add x0, x0, #15
    and x22, x0, #~15              // stack_adj
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_sub_rsp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_sub_rsp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rsp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rsp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x21, #6                     // k = 6
.Lx86mar_stack_loop:
    cmp x21, x20
    b.ge .Lx86mar_regs
    // movq slot(vreg_k), %r10
    lsl x0, x21, #1
    add x0, x0, #1                 // 1 + 2k → vreg field
    ldr x0, [x19, x0, lsl #3]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    // movq %r10, (k-6)*8(%rsp)
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    sub x0, x21, #6
    lsl x0, x0, #3                // (k-6)*8
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_paren_rsp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_paren_rsp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    add x21, x21, #1
    b .Lx86mar_stack_loop

.Lx86mar_regs:
    // Register phase: args 0..min(count,6)-1 into %rdi..%r9.
    mov x21, #0
.Lx86mar_reg_loop:
    cmp x21, x20
    b.ge .Lx86mar_done
    cmp x21, #6
    b.ge .Lx86mar_done
    lsl x0, x21, #1
    add x0, x0, #1                 // 1 + 2k → vreg field
    ldr x0, [x19, x0, lsl #3]      // vreg
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_argregs@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_argregs@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1, x21, lsl #3]      // argreg[k] string ptr
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    add x21, x21, #1
    b .Lx86mar_reg_loop

.Lx86mar_done:
    mov x0, x22                     // stack_adj
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_call_args(uint64_t *args) → stack_adj.  Reads the count from the
//    argument array header and marshals; a null array is a zero-argument call.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_call_args:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-16]!
    mov x29, sp
    cbz x0, .Lx86ca_none
    ldr x1, [x0]                    // word0 = count | (fsi << 32)
    and x1, x1, #0xffffffff         // count
    bl _kcc_x86_marshal             // marshal(args=x0, count=x1) → x0 = stack_adj
    b .Lx86ca_ret
.Lx86ca_none:
    mov x0, #0
.Lx86ca_ret:
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #16
    ret
@}

\subsection{The Dispatch}
\label{sec:x86-dispatch}

With the shape helpers in hand, \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_sel} is a flat opcode
switch.  \texttt{IR\_CONST} materialises its immediate with \texttt{movabsq}
(the one x86 move that takes a full 64-bit immediate) into \verb|%r10| and
stores it.  \texttt{IR\_ALLOCA} computes the \emph{address} of its storage---%
\verb|-(vreg_n + offset + size)*8(%rbp)|, where the pre-scan left the running
slot offset in \texttt{SRC2} and \texttt{SRC1} is the object's slot count---%
with a \texttt{leaq} and stores that pointer.  \texttt{IR\_RET} loads the
returned vreg into \verb|%rax| (the low 32 bits are the \texttt{int} result
the process exit status reads) and falls into the \texttt{leave}/\texttt{retq}
epilogue; a \texttt{void} return skips straight to it.  Everything else routes
to a shape helper.

An opcode the back end has \emph{not} yet learned---a floating-point operation,
inline assembly, a by-value aggregate---does not fall through silently.  A
genuine \texttt{IR\_NOP} emits nothing and is skipped, but any other unrecognised
opcode makes selection print a diagnostic and exit non-zero.  This is a
deliberate choice: emitting nothing for, say, an \texttt{IR\_FMUL} would leave
its destination slot holding garbage and \emph{miscompile} the program with no
warning---the worst possible failure.  Refusing cleanly instead keeps a
program that uses an unimplemented feature (a \texttt{double}-heavy interpreter
like Lua) an honest compile-time error rather than a wrong answer at run time,
matching the front end's treatment of VLAs and \texttt{\_Complex}.  As each
later rung teaches the back end a new opcode, the set that trips the refusal
shrinks.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_sel(ir_instr *i) — select x86-64 for one IR instruction.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_sel:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    ldr x22, [x19, #IR_OP]

    cmp x22, #IR_CONST
    b.eq .Lx86sel_const
    cmp x22, #IR_RET
    b.eq .Lx86sel_ret
    cmp x22, #IR_ALLOCA
    b.eq .Lx86sel_alloca
    cmp x22, #IR_LOAD
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load
    cmp x22, #IR_STORE
    b.eq .Lx86sel_store

    // Two-operand ALU: route by opcode to a mnemonic, then kcc_x86_binop.
    // IR_GEP is address arithmetic (base + byte offset), i.e. a plain addq.
    cmp x22, #IR_ADD
    b.eq .Lx86sel_add
    cmp x22, #IR_GEP
    b.eq .Lx86sel_add
    cmp x22, #IR_SUB
    b.eq .Lx86sel_sub
    cmp x22, #IR_MUL
    b.eq .Lx86sel_mul
    cmp x22, #IR_AND
    b.eq .Lx86sel_and
    cmp x22, #IR_OR
    b.eq .Lx86sel_or
    cmp x22, #IR_XOR
    b.eq .Lx86sel_xor
    cmp x22, #IR_NEG
    b.eq .Lx86sel_neg
    cmp x22, #IR_NOT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_not
    cmp x22, #IR_SHL
    b.eq .Lx86sel_shl
    cmp x22, #IR_LSHR
    b.eq .Lx86sel_lshr
    cmp x22, #IR_ASHR
    b.eq .Lx86sel_ashr
    cmp x22, #IR_SDIV
    b.eq .Lx86sel_sdiv
    cmp x22, #IR_UDIV
    b.eq .Lx86sel_udiv
    cmp x22, #IR_SMOD
    b.eq .Lx86sel_smod
    cmp x22, #IR_UMOD
    b.eq .Lx86sel_umod
    cmp x22, #IR_SEXT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_sext
    cmp x22, #IR_ZEXT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_zext
    cmp x22, #IR_TRUNC
    b.eq .Lx86sel_trunc
    cmp x22, #IR_BITCAST
    b.eq .Lx86sel_bitcast

    // Comparisons (rung 3): cmp + set<cc> + movzbl.
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_EQ
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpeq
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_NE
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpne
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_SLT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpslt
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_SLE
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpsle
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_SGT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpsgt
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_SGE
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpsge
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_ULT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpult
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_ULE
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpule

    // Control flow and calls (rung 3).
    cmp x22, #IR_BR
    b.eq .Lx86sel_br
    cmp x22, #IR_BR_COND
    b.eq .Lx86sel_brcond
    cmp x22, #IR_CALL
    b.eq .Lx86sel_call
    cmp x22, #IR_CALL_IND
    b.eq .Lx86sel_callind
    cmp x22, #IR_GLOBAL_REF
    b.eq .Lx86sel_globalref
    cmp x22, #IR_STRING_LIT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_strlit

    // A genuine no-op emits nothing and is safe to skip.
    cmp x22, #IR_NOP
    b.eq .Lx86sel_done

    // Any other opcode is a feature this back end does not yet implement
    // (floating point, inline asm, by-value aggregates, ...).  Silently
    // skipping it would emit a value-less slot and miscompile the program, so
    // refuse cleanly instead---the same discipline the front end applies to
    // VLAs and _Complex.  This is what keeps a double-using program (Lua) an
    // honest compile-time error rather than a wrong answer at run time.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_unsup@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_unsup@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_io_err
    bl _kcc_io_flush
    mov x0, #1
    bl _kcc_sys_exit

.Lx86sel_const:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movabsq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movabsq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done

.Lx86sel_alloca:
    // D = (vreg_n + offset + size) * 8.
    ldr x20, [x19, #IR_SRC2]        // offset (slots, from pre-scan)
    ldr x21, [x19, #IR_SRC1]        // size (slots)
    cbnz x21, .Lx86sel_alloca_sz
    mov x21, #1
.Lx86sel_alloca_sz:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_vreg_n@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_vreg_n@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    add x0, x0, x20
    add x0, x0, x21
    lsl x20, x0, #3                 // D (bytes)
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_leaq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_leaq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_x86_disp
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done

.Lx86sel_ret:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]
    cbz x0, .Lx86sel_ret_epi        // null type → void
    ldr x1, [x0, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x1, #TY_VOID
    b.eq .Lx86sel_ret_epi
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
.Lx86sel_ret_epi:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_leave@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_leave@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_retq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_retq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    b .Lx86sel_done

.Lx86sel_load:
    // Access-size override in SRC2 selects the extending move.
    ldr x20, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    cmp x20, #1
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_u8
    cmp x20, #2
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_u16
    cmp x20, #4
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_u32
    cmp x20, #5
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_s8
    cmp x20, #6
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_s16
    cmp x20, #7
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_s32
    cmp x20, #8
    b.eq .Lx86sel_done              // single-precision float: deferred
    // Default: 64-bit movq.
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_u8:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzbq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzbq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_u16:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzwq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzwq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_u32:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10d@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10d@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_s8:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movsbq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movsbq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_s16:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movswq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movswq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_s32:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movslq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movslq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done

.Lx86sel_store:
    // Access-size override in DEST selects the store width.
    ldr x20, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    cmp x20, #1
    b.eq .Lx86sel_store_8
    cmp x20, #2
    b.eq .Lx86sel_store_16
    cmp x20, #4
    b.eq .Lx86sel_store_32
    cmp x20, #8
    b.eq .Lx86sel_done              // single-precision float: deferred
    // Default: 64-bit movq.
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10_pr11@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10_pr11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memstore
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_store_8:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movb@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movb@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10b_pr11@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10b_pr11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memstore
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_store_16:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movw@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movw@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10w_pr11@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10w_pr11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memstore
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_store_32:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10d_pr11@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10d_pr11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memstore
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Two-operand ALU tails: load mnemonic, call the binop shape. ----
.Lx86sel_add:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_addq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_addq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_sub:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_subq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_subq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_mul:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_imulq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_imulq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_and:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_andq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_andq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_or:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_orq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_orq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_xor:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_xorq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_xorq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_neg:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_negq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_negq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_unop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_not:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_notq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_notq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_unop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_shl:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_shlq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_shlq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_shift
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_lshr:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_shrq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_shrq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_shift
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_ashr:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_sarq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_sarq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_shift
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_sdiv:
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, #1                      // signed
    mov x2, #0                      // quotient
    bl _kcc_x86_divop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_udiv:
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, #0
    mov x2, #0
    bl _kcc_x86_divop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_smod:
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, #1
    mov x2, #1                      // remainder
    bl _kcc_x86_divop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_umod:
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, #0
    mov x2, #1
    bl _kcc_x86_divop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_sext:
    mov x0, x19
    ldr x1, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // source width
    mov x2, #1                      // signed
    bl _kcc_x86_conv
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_zext:
    mov x0, x19
    ldr x1, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // source width
    mov x2, #0                      // unsigned
    bl _kcc_x86_conv
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_trunc:
    mov x0, x19
    ldr x1, [x19, #IR_TYPE]         // target type → width
    cbz x1, .Lx86sel_trunc_copy
    ldr x1, [x1, #TY_SIZE]
    mov x2, #0                      // truncation keeps low bytes, zero-extended
    bl _kcc_x86_conv
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_trunc_copy:
    mov x1, #8                      // unknown width → plain copy
    mov x2, #0
    bl _kcc_x86_conv
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_bitcast:
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, #8                      // plain movq copy
    mov x2, #0
    bl _kcc_x86_conv
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Comparisons: load the set<cc> mnemonic, call the cmp shape. ----
.Lx86sel_cmpeq:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_sete@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_sete@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpne:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setne@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setne@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpslt:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setl@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpsle:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setle@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setle@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpsgt:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setg@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setg@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpsge:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setge@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setge@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpult:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setb@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setb@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpule:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setbe@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setbe@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86sel_cmp_go:
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_x86_cmp
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Unconditional branch: jmp .Lkccbb<target>. ----
.Lx86sel_br:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_jmp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_jmp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // target BB id
    bl _kcc_x86_bblabel
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Conditional branch: test cond, jne true, jmp false. ----
.Lx86sel_brcond:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]         // condition vreg
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_test_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_test_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_jne@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_jne@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // true BB id
    bl _kcc_x86_bblabel
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_jmp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_jmp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // false BB id
    bl _kcc_x86_bblabel
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Global reference: leaq _<name>(%rip), %r10 ; store to dest. ----
.Lx86sel_globalref:
    // Load the symbol's address from the GOT: movq _<name>@@GOTPCREL(%rip), %r10.
    // The GOT form resolves uniformly for a symbol defined in this unit (the
    // linker points the slot at the local definition) and for an external one
    // imported from another object or a dylib (like puts), which has no fixed
    // image address---exactly the ARM64 back end's GOTPAGE discipline.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_us@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_us@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // symbol name
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_got_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_got_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- String literal: record (bytes,len), leaq .Lx86str<id>(%rip), %r10. ----
.Lx86sel_strlit:
    // id = current count of the string table; record {SRC1, SRC2}.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    ldr x20, [x0, #VEC_COUNT]       // id = current entry count
    ldr x1, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // data ptr
    ldr x2, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // length
    stp x1, x2, [sp, #-16]!         // build the {ptr, len} entry
    mov x1, sp
    bl _kcc_vec_push                // push(strs, &entry)
    add sp, sp, #16
    // leaq .Lx86str<id>(%rip), %r10
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_leaq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_leaq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_strlbl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_strlbl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20                     // id
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rip_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rip_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Direct call: marshal args, call _<name>, store %rax. ----
.Lx86sel_call:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // args array (0 if none)
    bl _kcc_x86_call_args           // loads args into ABI regs/stack
    mov x20, x0                     // x20 = stack_adj (survives the call sequence)
    // movb $0, %al   (variadic safety: no FP args in rung 3)
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_al@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_al@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    // call _<name>
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_call@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_call@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_us@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_us@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // callee name
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    b .Lx86sel_call_tail

// ---- Indirect call: marshal args, call *%r11, store %rax. ----
.Lx86sel_callind:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // args array
    bl _kcc_x86_call_args           // loads args into ABI regs/stack
    mov x20, x0                     // x20 = stack_adj
    // Load the function pointer into %r11 (arg regs already set; r11 is free).
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // fn-ptr vreg
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_al@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_al@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_call_r11@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_call_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    // fall through to the shared return/stack-restore tail

.Lx86sel_call_tail:
    // Restore the stack if arguments overflowed onto it (x20 = stack_adj).
    cbz x20, .Lx86sel_call_ret
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_add_rsp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_add_rsp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rsp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rsp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86sel_call_ret:
    // Store %rax into the result vreg unless the call is void.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]
    cbz x0, .Lx86sel_done           // no type → nothing to home
    ldr x1, [x0, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x1, #TY_VOID
    b.eq .Lx86sel_done
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done

.Lx86sel_done:
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret
@}

\section{The Frame: Pre-scan, Prologue, Epilogue}
\label{sec:x86-frame}

Before a function's instructions can be selected, its frame must be sized.
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_prescan} walks every basic block and, for each
\texttt{IR\_ALLOCA}, records the running slot offset in \texttt{SRC2} and
advances by the alloca's slot count (\texttt{SRC1}, defaulting to one).  It
returns the total number of alloca slots.  The vreg value slots and the alloca
storage together fix the frame size; the prologue emits the standard
\verb|pushq %rbp| / \verb|movq %rsp, %rbp| and, when the frame is non-empty,
one \verb|subq $frame, %rsp| (rounded up to a 16-byte multiple so the stack
stays aligned).  The \texttt{leave} in every \texttt{IR\_RET} epilogue undoes
all of it in one instruction, so no per-function epilogue state is needed.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_prescan(ir_func *f) → total alloca slots; sets each alloca's SRC2.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_prescan:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-64]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    mov x19, x0                     // func
    mov x20, #0                     // running offset (slots)
    ldr x21, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    cbz x21, .Lx86ps_done
    ldr x22, [x21, #VEC_COUNT]
    mov x23, #0                     // bb index
.Lx86ps_bb:
    cmp x23, x22
    b.ge .Lx86ps_done
    mov x0, x21
    mov x1, x23
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x24, [x0]                   // BB ptr
    ldr x24, [x24, #BB_FIRST]
.Lx86ps_instr:
    cbz x24, .Lx86ps_next_bb
    ldr x0, [x24, #IR_OP]
    cmp x0, #IR_ALLOCA
    b.ne .Lx86ps_skip
    str x20, [x24, #IR_SRC2]        // record this alloca's offset
    ldr x0, [x24, #IR_SRC1]         // slot count
    cbnz x0, .Lx86ps_add
    mov x0, #1
.Lx86ps_add:
    add x20, x20, x0
.Lx86ps_skip:
    ldr x24, [x24, #IR_NEXT]
    b .Lx86ps_instr
.Lx86ps_next_bb:
    add x23, x23, #1
    b .Lx86ps_bb
.Lx86ps_done:
    mov x0, x20
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #64
    ret
@}

\section{Spilling Incoming Parameters}
\label{sec:x86-params}

On the callee side, the System~V ABI delivers the first six integer parameters
in \verb|%rdi|--\verb|%r9| and the rest on the caller's stack.  Because the IR
numbers a function's parameters as its first vregs (\texttt{\%0}, \texttt{\%1},
\ldots), the prologue copies each incoming register into its parameter's vreg
slot, after which a parameter is indistinguishable from any other value in the
all-spill model.  A parameter past the sixth is read from the caller's stack
image at \verb|16 + (k-6)*8(%rbp)| (the saved \verb|%rbp| and return address
occupy the first sixteen bytes above \verb|%rbp|).  As with the argument
marshalling, rung~three assumes every parameter is of the integer class;
floating-point and by-value aggregate parameters are a later rung.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_spill_params(ir_func *f) — copy incoming args into vreg slots.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_spill_params:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldr x19, [x0, #IRF_PARAMS]      // param count
    mov x20, #0                     // k
.Lx86sp_loop:
    cmp x20, x19
    b.ge .Lx86sp_done
    cmp x20, #6
    b.ge .Lx86sp_stack
    // movq %argreg[k], slot(k)
    mov x0, x20                     // vreg = k
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_argregs@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_argregs@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1, x20, lsl #3]       // argreg[k] string ptr
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sp_next
.Lx86sp_stack:
    // movq 16+(k-6)*8(%rbp), %r10   (positive displacement: caller stack image)
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    sub x0, x20, #6
    lsl x0, x0, #3
    add x0, x0, #16                 // 16 + (k-6)*8
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rbp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rbp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    // store %r10 → slot(k)
    mov x0, x20
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
.Lx86sp_next:
    add x20, x20, #1
    b .Lx86sp_loop
.Lx86sp_done:
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\section{Walking the Module}
\label{sec:x86-walk}

The top-level walk mirrors the IR dumper (\S\ref{sec:ir-dump}): iterate the
function list \texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_funcs}, and for each function emit its symbol
declaration and label, publish its vreg count, run the pre-scan, emit the
prologue, then stream its instructions through selection.  A
non-\texttt{static} function gets a \texttt{.globl}; the label is always the
name with the Mach-O leading underscore.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_render_text() — walk the IR, emit x86-64 assembly to the buffer.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_render_text:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-96]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    stp x25, x26, [sp, #64]
    str x27, [sp, #80]

    // Section directive.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_text@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_text@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str

    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_funcs@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_funcs@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x19, [x0]
    cbz x19, .Lx86rt_done
    ldr x20, [x19, #VEC_COUNT]      // func count
    mov x21, #0                     // func index

.Lx86rt_func:
    cmp x21, x20
    b.ge .Lx86rt_done
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, x21
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x22, [x0]                   // func ptr

    // .globl _<name>   (unless static)
    ldr x0, [x22, #IRF_STATIC]
    cbnz x0, .Lx86rt_label
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_globl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_globl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x22, #IRF_NAME]
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, #10                     // '\n'
    bl _kcc_as_ch

.Lx86rt_label:
    // _<name>:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_us@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_us@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x22, #IRF_NAME]
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_colon_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_colon_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str

    // Publish the vreg count (alloca addressing reads it) and pre-scan allocas.
    ldr x26, [x22, #IRF_VREG_N]     // vreg count
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_vreg_n@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_vreg_n@@PAGEOFF
    str x26, [x0]
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_x86_prescan             // x0 = total alloca slots

    // frame_bytes = align16((vreg_count + alloca_slots) * 8).
    add x0, x0, x26
    lsl x0, x0, #3                  // bytes
    add x0, x0, #15
    and x0, x0, #~15
    mov x27, x0                     // frame_bytes

    // Prologue: pushq %rbp ; movq %rsp, %rbp ; subq $frame, %rsp.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_push_rbp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_push_rbp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_mov_sp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_mov_sp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    cbz x27, .Lx86rt_body
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_sub_sp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_sub_sp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x27
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_sp_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_sp_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str

.Lx86rt_body:
    // Spill incoming parameters into their vreg slots (SysV integer class).
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_x86_spill_params

    // Walk basic blocks.
    ldr x23, [x22, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    cbz x23, .Lx86rt_next_func
    ldr x24, [x23, #VEC_COUNT]      // block count
    mov x25, #0                     // block index

.Lx86rt_bb:
    cmp x25, x24
    b.ge .Lx86rt_next_func
    mov x0, x23
    mov x1, x25
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x27, [x0]                   // BB ptr
    // Emit the block's label ".Lkccbb<id>:" so branches can target it.
    ldr x0, [x27, #BB_ID]
    bl _kcc_x86_bblabel
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_label_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_label_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x27, [x27, #BB_FIRST]       // first instruction

.Lx86rt_instr:
    cbz x27, .Lx86rt_next_bb
    mov x0, x27
    bl _kcc_x86_sel
    ldr x27, [x27, #IR_NEXT]
    b .Lx86rt_instr

.Lx86rt_next_bb:
    add x25, x25, #1
    b .Lx86rt_bb

.Lx86rt_next_func:
    add x21, x21, #1
    b .Lx86rt_func

.Lx86rt_done:
    ldr x27, [sp, #80]
    ldp x25, x26, [sp, #64]
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #96
    ret
@}

\section{Emitting Global Variable Storage}
\label{sec:x86-data-emit}

Functions are only half of a translation unit; the other half is its
file-scope data.  \kcc{} emits a \emph{mutable} global's initialiser as
runtime stores (they were streamed into \texttt{main} above, exactly like any
other assignment), so the storage itself only has to be reserved and
zeroed---an initialised store then overwrites the zero with the real value.
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_emit\_data} walks the AST translation unit
(\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_tu}, published by the driver) and, for each defining
variable declaration, emits a \verb|.zerofill __DATA,__common| reservation at
the declared type's size (floored at eight bytes and rounded to a whole
eight-byte slot, matching the ARM64 pre-registration).  An external global
(\texttt{extern}) is a declaration, not a definition, and is skipped; a
\texttt{static} file-scope global gets no \texttt{.globl} (internal linkage);
everything else is exported so other units can link against it.

One class is deliberately \emph{not} emitted here: a \texttt{const} global
whose initialiser is a fixed byte image
(\texttt{\_kcc\_global\_const\_rodata}/\texttt{\_ptrarr})---\kcc{} realises
those as read-only data with their actual bytes and emits \emph{no} runtime
store, so zeroed storage would read as zero rather than the initialiser.  Until
the read-only-data rung lands, such a global is left undefined (a link error,
not a silent wrong value).  This pass also walks only \emph{top-level}
declarations, so a block-scoped \texttt{static} local---which \kcc{} hoists to a
mangled \texttt{sl\_}\meta{node} global reached by \texttt{IR\_GLOBAL\_REF}---is
likewise still undefined; reserving its storage means recursing into function
bodies, as the ARM64 pre-registration's second pass does, and is deferred to
the same later rung.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_emit_data() — emit .zerofill storage for file-scope globals.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_data:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_tu@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_tu@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x19, [x0]
    cbz x19, .Lx86ed_done
    ldr x19, [x19, #AST_F0]         // first top-level declaration
.Lx86ed_loop:
    cbz x19, .Lx86ed_done
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x0, #AST_VAR_DECL
    b.ne .Lx86ed_next
    ldr x20, [x19, #AST_F1]         // name
    cbz x20, .Lx86ed_next
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F3]          // storage class
    cmp x0, #2                      // extern → declaration, no storage
    b.eq .Lx86ed_next
    cmp x0, #1                      // typedef → no storage
    b.eq .Lx86ed_next
    mov x21, x0                     // x21 = storage class (3 = static)
    // Skip const globals that become read-only byte images (deferred rung).
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_global_const_rodata
    cbnz x0, .Lx86ed_next
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_global_const_ptrarr
    cbnz x0, .Lx86ed_next
    // Size = round_up_8(max(8, type_layout(resolve(AST_F0)))).
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F0]
    bl _kcc_sema_resolve_type
    cbz x0, .Lx86ed_next
    bl _kcc_type_layout
    mov x22, x0
    cmp x22, #8
    b.ge .Lx86ed_round
    mov x22, #8
.Lx86ed_round:
    add x22, x22, #7
    and x22, x22, #~7               // round up to a whole 8-byte slot

    // .globl _<name>   (unless static: internal linkage)
    cmp x21, #3
    b.eq .Lx86ed_zf
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_globl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_globl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
.Lx86ed_zf:
    // .zerofill __DATA,__common,_<name>,<size>,3
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zerofill@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zerofill@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma1@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma1@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zf_a3@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zf_a3@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86ed_next:
    ldr x19, [x19, #AST_F4]         // next sibling
    b .Lx86ed_loop
.Lx86ed_done:
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret
@}

\section{Emitting String-Literal Data}
\label{sec:x86-strings}

A string literal reaches the back end as an \texttt{IR\_STRING\_LIT} whose
\texttt{SRC1} points at the raw bytes and \texttt{SRC2} is their length.
Selection cannot emit the bytes inline---they belong in the read-only
\texttt{\_\_TEXT,\_\_cstring} section, not in the instruction stream---so it
does the same two-phase trick the ARM64 back end does: at the use site it
assigns the literal the next private label \verb|.Lx86str|$N$, records the
\texttt{(bytes,\,length)} pair in a table, and loads the label's address with a
\texttt{leaq}.  After all functions and globals are emitted,
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_emit\_strings} walks the table once and emits the cstring
section: each entry's label followed by a \texttt{.byte} line carrying its
bytes and a terminating NUL.  Emitting raw \texttt{.byte} values (rather than a
quoted \texttt{.asciz}) sidesteps any escaping question---an embedded quote,
backslash, or NUL is just another number.  The table is per-render state,
initialised in \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_render}; the label is private
(\verb|.L|-prefixed), so two translation units never collide.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_emit_strings() — emit the cstring section for recorded literals.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_strings:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-64]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x19, [x0]
    cbz x19, .Lx86es_done
    ldr x20, [x19, #VEC_COUNT]      // number of literals
    cbz x20, .Lx86es_done

    // .section __TEXT,__cstring,cstring_literals
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cstrsect@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cstrsect@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str

    mov x21, #0                     // literal index
.Lx86es_lit:
    cmp x21, x20
    b.ge .Lx86es_done
    // .Lx86str<id>:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_strlbl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_strlbl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_label_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_label_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    // Fetch entry {ptr, len}.
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, x21
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x23, [x0]                   // data ptr
    ldr x24, [x0, #8]               // length
    // \t.byte
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_byte@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_byte@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x22, #0                     // byte index
.Lx86es_byte:
    cmp x22, x24
    b.ge .Lx86es_nul
    ldrb w0, [x23, x22]
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma1@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma1@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    add x22, x22, #1
    b .Lx86es_byte
.Lx86es_nul:
    // trailing NUL terminator, then newline
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    add x21, x21, #1
    b .Lx86es_lit
.Lx86es_done:
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #64
    ret
@}

\section{Rendering to a File}
\label{sec:x86-render}

\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_render(path)} is the entry point the driver calls.  It
reuses the growable text buffer of the \texttt{.s} back end
(\S\ref{sec:emit-asm-buffer})---\texttt{\_kcc\_as\_init} to allocate it and
\texttt{\_kcc\_as\_str}/\texttt{\_kcc\_as\_ch}/\texttt{\_kcc\_as\_dec} to fill
it---renders the functions and then the global data, then writes the buffer to
the output path through the shared \texttt{\_kcc\_as\_write\_file} helper,
exactly as the ARM64 assembly path does.  The buffer machinery is
target-neutral; only the text poured into it is x86-64.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_render(const char *path) — render x86-64 asm to <path>. 0 on ok.
.globl _kcc_x86_render
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_render:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    str x0, [sp, #16]              // save path across the render calls
    bl _kcc_as_init
    // Fresh string-literal table for this render (16-byte {ptr,len} entries).
    mov x0, #16
    bl _kcc_vec_init
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGEOFF
    str x0, [x1]
    bl _kcc_x86_render_text        // emits functions (records string literals)
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_data          // file-scope global storage
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_strings       // cstring section for recorded literals
    ldr x0, [sp, #16]             // path
    bl _kcc_as_write_file
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@| _kcc_x86_render _kcc_target _kcc_x86_tu
@}

\section{Rung One in Retrospect}
\label{sec:x86-rung1-history}

Rung~one selected only \texttt{IR\_CONST} and \texttt{IR\_RET} and carried the
returned constant in a small per-function value map, emitting the whole
function as \verb|movl $N, %eax| / \verb|retq| with no stack frame at all.
Rung~two subsumes that path: a constant now materialises into its vreg slot
and the return loads from it, so \verb|int main(void){return N;}| still exits
with \texttt{N}---now through the general all-spill frame rather than a
special case.  The map is gone; every value lives in memory between
instructions, which is exactly what let the arithmetic and memory opcodes join
the target without disturbing the ones already working.
